WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.610
The Mandelbrot Set

00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:03.966
has many intriguing features.

00:00:04.066 --> 00:00:05.319
But its biggest mysteries

00:00:05.319 --> 00:00:08.866
lie in its complex fractal boundary.

00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:11.218
Zooming in to different boundary regions

00:00:11.218 --> 00:00:14.100
reveals some astounding features...

00:00:14.166 --> 00:00:16.900
a valley of seahorses,

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:19.333
parades of elephants

00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:22.133
and miniature versions of the set itself.

00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:23.478
So we can sort of keep finding

00:00:23.478 --> 00:00:26.016
nests, a sequence of smaller and smaller

00:00:26.016 --> 00:00:27.138
Mandelbrot sets,

00:00:27.138 --> 00:00:29.166
all one inside of the next.

00:00:29.266 --> 00:00:30.343
Here at the boundary,

00:00:30.343 --> 00:00:33.266
mathematicians are probing for answers.

00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:34.100
As you look at it

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:35.466
it sort of seems like one blob,

00:00:35.466 --> 00:00:36.849
but if you really ask like,

00:00:36.849 --> 00:00:37.939
is it all connected?

00:00:37.939 --> 00:00:39.226
Or maybe if I zoom in far enough,

00:00:39.226 --> 00:00:40.700
there's some separating piece.

00:00:40.933 --> 00:00:42.694
This question is central

00:00:42.694 --> 00:00:43.890
to a 40 year old problem

00:00:43.890 --> 00:00:45.033
called the Mandelbrot

00:00:45.066 --> 00:00:46.687
Locally Connected Conjecture,

00:00:46.687 --> 00:00:48.833
or MLC.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:50.645
If the set is locally connected,

00:00:50.645 --> 00:00:52.642
that means that no matter what point

00:00:52.642 --> 00:00:54.021
you choose to examine

00:00:54.021 --> 00:00:55.566
or how far you zoom in,

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:57.755
the area will always look like

00:00:57.755 --> 00:00:59.766
one nice connected section.

